  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Thursday)\nIssued at 1250 PM CDT FRI AUG 28 2026\n\nThe short-term weather pattern will be heavily influenced by an\nupper-level shortwave trough currently tracking at a slow pace\nalong the Gulf Coast region. At the surface, a prevailing\nnortherly flow will facilitate the steady advection of drier,\ncooler air into the northern portions of our forecast area,\neffectively limiting moisture availability in that region.\nConsequently, the highest PoPs over the next 12 to 36 hours will\nremain strictly confined to the southern portions of Central\nAlabama, with a focus on the southeastern counties where moisture\nlevels remain higher. While diurnal instability will prove\nsufficient to support convective development, the presence of\nweak vertical wind shear suggests that storm organization will be\nlimited, keeping the overall severe threat low. However, a\nconditional threat for wet microbursts remains a concern from the\nCalera area southward the I-85 corridor, specifically contingent\non the development of enough localized surface-based instability\nto fuel downdrafts. In the far southern section, expect to see\nscattered, slow-moving convective cells developing throughout the\nafternoon hours.\n\nLooking ahead, model guidance shows improved agreement regarding\nthe initial handling of the stubborn upper-level low. A broad,\nhigh-amplitude ridge is projected to build and establish a\nsignificant east-west axis stretching from Arkansas eastward to\nthe Carolinas by early next week. This ridge will ultimately\nforce the cutoff low to be steered westward by the prevailing\ntropical easterlies toward the Mississippi River, effectively\ndiminishing its influence on our regional weather. We maintain\nmoderate confidence (60%) that this developing pattern will\nsuccessfully suppress daily precipitation chances to levels below\nclimatological normals, while simultaneously elevating afternoon\nhigh temperatures. As a result, we may need to monitor the\nsituation for the potential issuance of additional Heat\nAdvisories.\n\n16",
